[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: Router6’s packet filter (iptables) FORWARD chain. Mechanism: Traffic from Location A toward Location C traverses R0 → R1 → R2 → R3 and then reaches Router6 on its uplink (eth2, 10.50.1.2). On Router6, any packet arriving on eth2 is dropped by explicit FORWARD rules, so traceroutes/pings from PC4/PC5 time out when they hit Router6.

Evidence and localization:
- PC4 can reach its default gateway (172.16.10.1) and other remote sites (e.g., 192.168.200.11), but cannot reach 172.18.51.18 (Location C host). Traceroute from PC4 stalls after hop 5 at 10.50.1.2 (Router6).
- Router6 can ping 172.18.51.18 locally, proving the LAN and host are fine.
- Router0 and Router3 cannot ping 172.18.51.18, matching the drop at Router6’s ingress.
- Router6 iptables shows:
  -A FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP (plus specific TCP/80 exception to 172.18.51.1). Counters show packets being dropped on these rules. IP forwarding is enabled, so policy, not routing, is the blocker.

Root cause: Misconfigured iptables on Router6 that drops all forwarded traffic arriving on eth2, unintentionally isolating Location C from the rest of the network.

Corrective fix: On router6, remove or adjust the eth2 DROP rules. For example:
- iptables -F FORWARD
- Then add explicit allows, e.g., iptables -A FORWARD -i eth2 -d 172.18.50.0/23 -j ACCEPT and symmetric return rules, or set ACCEPT as default as intended.
Ensure persistence (e.g., save rules or disable any offending firewall service/script that re-adds these drops). After this, PC4/PC5 should reach Location C normally. 
=== END ===